EXSECUTOR LICENSE EXCEPTIONS
Version 2.0, 2026-09-09

Additional permissions under section 7 of the GNU General Public License,
version 3, as published by the Free Software Foundation.

These exceptions accompany the LICENSE file in this repository and form part of
the terms under which Exsecutor is distributed. Where an exception grants a
permission the GPL would otherwise withhold, the exception controls.

The intent, stated plainly before the terms: **the Exsecutor compiler is free
software and must stay free. What you write in Exsecutor is yours.** Compiling
a program with `exsc` places no licensing obligation on that program, and
linking a designated runtime file into it places none either.

There are two distinct exceptions here, and they solve two different problems.
Exception A is automatic and covers what the compiler *produces*. Exception B
is per-file and covers what gets *linked into* your program.


===========================================================================
EXCEPTION A -- COMPILER OUTPUT
===========================================================================

Modelled on the FAUST compiler's GPL notice, the GCC Runtime Library
Exception, and the Bison parser exception.

A1. DEFINITIONS

"The Compiler" means the Exsecutor compiler `exsc`, together with everything in
this repository used to build, test, or verify it.

"Compiler Output" means any work produced by running the Compiler on input you
supply: emitted C source, generated assembly, object code, executables,
interface (`ego`) files, diagnostics, and any intermediate representation the
Compiler writes out.

A2. GRANT

You have permission to propagate Compiler Output under terms of your choosing.
Compiler Output is not a work based on the Compiler, and you are not required
to license it under the GNU General Public License.

Your program does not become a work based on the Compiler merely by having been
compiled with it.

A3. LIMIT

This exception does not permit distributing a modified Compiler under terms
other than the GPL. If you change the Compiler and distribute it, the GPL
applies to your changes.


===========================================================================
EXCEPTION B -- LINKING ("CLASSPATH-STYLE")
===========================================================================

Modelled on the GNU Classpath exception, as used by OpenJDK. The mechanism is
deliberately the same: the exception attaches to **individually designated
files**, not to a category described in prose.

B1. THE EXCEPTION

  Linking this file statically or dynamically with other modules is making a
  combined work based on this file. Thus, the terms and conditions of the GNU
  General Public License cover the whole combination.

  As a special exception, the copyright holders of this file give you
  permission to link this file with independent modules to produce an
  executable, regardless of the license terms of these independent modules, and
  to copy and distribute the resulting executable under terms of your choice,
  provided that you also meet, for each linked independent module, the terms
  and conditions of the license of that module. An independent module is a
  module which is not derived from or based on this file. If you modify this
  file, you may extend this exception to your version of the file, but you are
  not obligated to do so. If you do not wish to do so, delete this exception
  statement from your version.

B2. HOW A FILE BECOMES SUBJECT TO EXCEPTION B

A file is subject to Exception B **if and only if its own header says so**, in
these words:

    This particular file is designated as subject to the "Linking" exception
    as provided in the LICENSE.EXCEPTION file that accompanies this code.

No file is covered by Exception B by category, by directory, by resemblance, or
by anyone's later interpretation of what a "runtime" is. Designation is
explicit, per file, and visible in the file itself.

This is the whole reason for adopting OpenJDK's mechanism rather than defining
the boundary in prose. An earlier version of this document tried to describe
the excepted set as "the target runtime," which required a reader to correctly
distinguish two different things that share a word -- see B3. A designation
line cannot be misread.

B3. THE DISTINCTION THIS MECHANISM EXISTS TO REMOVE

The word "runtime" names two different things in this repository:

  compiler/x86_64/rt/    The COMPILER's own internals -- arena allocator,
                         vector, insertion-ordered map, string interner,
                         syscall wrappers. Statically linked into the `exsc`
                         binary. Never emitted into Compiler Output, never
                         linked into your program.

                         NOT designated. Plain GPL. No exception.

  the target runtime     Reference-counting and memory-management support
                         (specification section 6), and standard library
                         (`norma.*`) modules the Compiler links or emits into
                         a compiled program.

                         DESIGNATED, file by file, once it exists.

At the time of writing **no target runtime exists** -- the compiler is under
construction and nothing is emitted into user programs. No file in this
repository currently carries a designation line. Exception B is written forward
so that the licensing position is settled before that code lands, rather than
renegotiated afterwards once contributors hold copyright in it.

B4. LIMIT

Exception B does not permit taking a designated file and using it with a
compiler other than the Compiler in order to escape that compiler's own terms.


===========================================================================
FILE HEADERS
===========================================================================

Two forms. Every project-authored source file carries one of them.

--- FORM 1: compiler files (the default; no Exception B) -------------------

    ; <path>
    ; SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
    ; Copyright (C) 2026 The Exsecutor authors.
    ;
    ; DO NOT ALTER OR REMOVE COPYRIGHT NOTICES OR THIS FILE HEADER.
    ;
    ; This code is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify it
    ; under the terms of the GNU General Public License as published by the
    ; Free Software Foundation, either version 3 of the License, or (at your
    ; option) any later version.
    ;
    ; This code is distributed in the hope that it will be useful, but WITHOUT
    ; ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of MERCHANTABILITY or
    ; FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE. See the GNU General Public License for
    ; more details.
    ;
    ; You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License along
    ; with this code. If not, see <https://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
    ;
    ; Code produced by this compiler is not covered by the GPL --
    ; see Exception A in LICENSE.EXCEPTION.

--- FORM 2: designated files (Exception B applies) -------------------------

Identical to Form 1, with one additional paragraph after the first, exactly as
OpenJDK designates its own files:

    ; This particular file is designated as subject to the "Linking" exception
    ; as provided in the LICENSE.EXCEPTION file that accompanies this code.

Use `#` rather than `;` as the comment leader in shell and Python sources, and
the SPDX identifier for a designated file is:

    SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later WITH Classpath-exception-2.0


===========================================================================
THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS
===========================================================================

`vendor/` contains third-party code that is **not** covered by the GPL or by
either exception, and is redistributed under its own terms:

  vendor/fasmg-x86/   flat assembler g (fasmg) x86 macro package
                      Copyright (c) Tomasz Grysztar
                      BSD 3-Clause -- see vendor/fasmg-x86/LICENSE.txt
                      Provenance and hashes: vendor/fasmg-x86/PROVENANCE.md

BSD 3-Clause is GPL-compatible, so the combined work distributes cleanly. That
directory keeps its own license and its own copyright notice, and must not be
relicensed or have its notices stripped.


===========================================================================
NO WARRANTY, AND NOT LEGAL ADVICE
===========================================================================

The disclaimer of warranty in sections 15 and 16 of the GPL applies to these
exceptions as it does to the rest of the distribution.

Exception B's operative paragraph (B1) is the GNU Classpath exception, adapted
only in that it says "this file" where the original says "this library" --
matching OpenJDK's per-file designation practice. Exception A was drafted by
reference to FAUST, GCC and Bison. Neither has been reviewed by a lawyer. If
the licensing position of this project comes to matter commercially, have
counsel read it before relying on it.
